==Description== <!--T:3-->

<!--T:8-->
The [[Image:Sketcher_Dimension.svg|24px]] [[Sketcher_Dimension|Sketcher Dimension]] tool is the context-sensitive constraint tool of the Sketcher Workbench. Based on the current selection, it offers appropriate dimensional constraints, but also geometric constraints.

==Usage== <!--T:5-->

<!--T:9-->
See also: [[Sketcher_Workbench#Drawing_aids|Drawing aids]].

<!--T:10-->
# Optionally select one or more elements (edges or points). [[Sketcher_Workbench#Sketcher_CompCreateBSpline|B-spline]] edges are currently not supported.
# There are several ways to invoke the tool:
#* If the {{MenuCommand|Dimensioning constraints}} [[Sketcher_Preferences#General|preference]] is set to {{Value|Both}} or {{Value|Single tool}} (default): press the {{Button|[[Image:Sketcher_Dimension.svg|16px]] [[Sketcher_Dimension|Dimension]]}} button.
#* Select the {{MenuCommand|Sketch → Constraints → [[Image:Sketcher_Dimension.svg|16px]] Dimension}} option from the menu.
#* Right-click in the [[3D_View|3D View]] and select the {{MenuCommand|Dimension → [[Image:Sketcher_Dimension.svg|16px]] Dimension}} option from the context menu.
#* If there is a selection: Right-click in the 3D View and select the {{MenuCommand|[[Image:Sketcher_Dimension.svg|16px]] Dimension}} option from the context menu.
#* Use the keyboard shortcut: {{KEY|D}}.
# The cursor changes to a cross with the tool icon.
# If you have not yet selected an element: select one.
# Depending on the selected element(s) a constraint is proposed.
# Optionally select an additional element.
# Optionally deselect an element by clicking it again.
# The proposed constraint is updated after every selection change.
# Optionally press the {{KEY|M}} key one or more times to cycle through other available constraints, if any.
# If a geometric constraint is proposed, selected elements may change giving a preview of the result.
# Do one of the following:
#* Click in an empty area in the [[3D_View|3D View]] to confirm:
#*# If a dimensional constraint is created the clicked point determines its location. For a linear dimension the clicked point also determines its type: [[Image:Sketcher_ConstrainDistanceX.svg|16px]] [[Sketcher_ConstrainDistanceX|horizontal dimension]], [[Image:Sketcher_ConstrainDistanceY.svg|16px]] [[Sketcher_ConstrainDistanceY|vertical dimension]] or  [[Image:Sketcher_ConstrainDistance.svg|16px]] [[Sketcher_ConstrainDistance|distance dimension]].
#*# If a [[Sketcher_ToggleDrivingConstraint|driving dimensional constraint]] is created, depending on the [[Sketcher_Preferences#Display|preferences]], a dialog opens to [[Sketcher_Workbench#Edit_constraints|edit its value]].
#*# A constraint is added.
#*# This tool always runs in continue mode: optionally keep creating constraints.
#* To finish, right-click or press {{KEY|Esc}}, or start another geometry or constraint creation tool.

=== External references ===

{{Version|26.3}}: Start the Dimension tool before selecting an edge or vertex of another visible object in the [[3D_View|3D View]]. The tool projects the selected element onto the sketch plane as external construction geometry. An existing reference to that element is reused. You do not need to run [[Sketcher_Projection|External Projection]] first.

To dimension a sketch point relative to a model vertex:
# Open the sketch in edit mode and make the source object visible.
# Start the Dimension tool, then select the sketch point and the model vertex.
# Move the cursor to position the dimension. For a linear dimension, its position determines whether the tool proposes a horizontal, vertical or distance constraint, as described above.
# Click in an empty area to confirm. Enter a value if the dimension dialog opens.

* Only individual edges and vertices can be selected this way. To project all edges of a face, use [[Sketcher_Projection|External Projection]].
* The source must be in the same document and must not depend on the sketch. For a sketch in a [[PartDesign_Body|Body]], the source must be in that Body. Cross-[[App_Part|Part]] references are not allowed. Use a [[PartDesign_SubShapeBinder|Sub-Shape Binder]] when a reference from another container is required.
* Press {{KEY|Esc}} or right-click to cancel the current operation. Newly created references for an unconfirmed dimension are removed; references used by earlier confirmed dimensions are retained.

==Notes== <!--T:11-->

<!--T:12-->
* [[Image:Sketcher_ConstrainDistanceX.svg|16px]] [[Sketcher_ConstrainDistanceX|Horizontal dimensions]] and [[Image:Sketcher_ConstrainDistanceY.svg|16px]] [[Sketcher_ConstrainDistanceY|vertical dimensions]] have a distinct behavior compared to [[Image:Sketcher_ConstrainDistance.svg|16px]] [[Sketcher_ConstrainDistance|distance dimensions]]. The first two dimension types have signed dimension values, they also constrain the relative positions of the measured points. These will only [[Sketcher_Workbench#Flipping|switch places]] if the sign of the dimension value is changed. For example, if the value of a horizontal dimension remains positive, or negative, the left point will always remain the left point. This is not the case for the distance dimension type, which has an unsigned value. {{VersionMinus|1.1}}: When using this tool to apply a dimensional constraint to points with the same X- or Y-coordinate, you need to take care when picking the point to position it. You can verify which dimension type will be created by checking the last item in the [[Sketcher_Dialog#Constraints|Constraints section of the Sketcher Dialog]], this will dynamically update as you move the mouse.


